From: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8280xp: add CPU cooling maps

The whole of sc8280xp.dtsi contains exactly one cooling-maps block, and it is
under gpu-thermal. Every cpuN-thermal zone has a single 110 degC critical trip
and nothing else, so the CPUs run flat out until the thermal core hits that
trip and the kernel performs an emergency shutdown. There is no gradual
throttling anywhere in between.

That is reachable on a passively cooled machine. On a Huawei MateBook E Go
(fanless 12.35" tablet, no vents) a long gaming session has nothing standing
between it and an abrupt power-off.

Add a passive trip at 85 degC to each of the eight per-core zones -- the same
temperature gpu-thermal already uses -- and bind it to the cpufreq cooling
device of that core's own cluster. All eight CPU nodes already declare
#cooling-cells = <2>, so nothing else is needed.

Each map lists all four CPUs of the cluster, which is the idiom sm8250 and
friends use: cpufreq cooling registers one device per policy, and the thermal
core picks whichever of the listed nodes owns it.

polling-delay-passive = <250> mirrors gpu-thermal so step_wise can react.

The 0-3 / 4-7 cluster split is confirmed on hardware: policy0 tops out at
2.44 GHz and policy4 at 2.69 GHz.

Verified by decompiling the built DTB: cooling-maps blocks go from 1 to 10 and
all eight cpuN_alert0 phandles resolve.

Until this patch the port carried a userspace step-wise governor writing
/sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/cur_state to cover the gap.

Signed-off-by: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
